Terraform is an open-source infrastructure-as-code tool that lets you declare what infrastructure you want, rather than writing procedural steps to build it. In Terraform, your configuration describes desired infrastructure, and Terraform figures out the sequence of actions needed to reach that target.

Terraform configurations are written in HashiCorp Configuration Language, commonly called HCL. Think of HCL as the “blueprint text” for infrastructure: it states resources, relationships, and settings, while Terraform handles the mechanics of turning that blueprint into real changes.

A key part of Terraform’s design is the provider. Providers translate Terraform configuration into API operations for external platforms, such as AWS, Docker, MinIO-compatible S3, or Kubernetes. This separation matters because Terraform stays consistent across platforms, while providers supply the platform-specific implementation details.

Terraform manages infrastructure objects using two main concepts: resources and data sources. Resources represent infrastructure objects that Terraform manages, such as a container or a network component. Data sources read existing information that your configuration needs, without necessarily managing the underlying object.

Terraform also builds a dependency graph from references between objects. When one resource depends on another, Terraform uses those dependencies to determine safe operation ordering, so it can create or update items in the right sequence.

Here is a minimal example that shows a Docker provider context with a resource and real attributes for a container. 
```hcl
terraform {
  required_providers {
    docker = {
      source  = "kreuzwerker/docker"
      version = "3.0.2"
    }
  }
}

provider "docker" {}

resource "docker_image" "nginx" {
  name = "nginx:latest"
}

resource "docker_container" "web" {
  name  = "example-web"
  image = docker_image.nginx.name
  ports {
    internal = 80
    external = 8080
  }
}
```

To compare resource versus data source, imagine you manage a container as a resource, but you read an existing value such as an image tag or endpoint as a data source. In both cases, the provider context is what enables Terraform to call the external platform APIs, while resources change infrastructure and data sources only retrieve information.
